INT. VILLAGE SCHOOLHOUSE - AFTER HOURS - DUSK

Rows of small desks. A globe with a dent in Africa. Chalk dust drifting through low gold light. A map of a country that no longer exists.

Three adults sit at children's desks, knees too high, teacups on the writing slopes.

HEADMISTRESS GWENDOLEN BEAUMONT-OKORO (60s, spectacles on a chain, a voice like a closing door) sits at the front. MR. ABEBE LINDQVIST-NWOSU (40s, the caretaker, a ring of keys), crammed behind a desk, drinks from a chipped mug. MRS. FATIMA KOWALSKI-ASANTE (30s, the only teacher left) rubs her temples.

GWENDOLEN
The inspector arrives Monday.

FATIMA
We know.

GWENDOLEN
He will want to see the registers.

ABEBE
They're in the cellar.

GWENDOLEN
Why?

ABEBE
Because of the flood.

GWENDOLEN
Mr. Lindqvist-Nwosu, the flood was in April.

ABEBE
They're still drying.

FATIMA
They're mold, Abebe.

ABEBE
Mold is a kind of record.

GWENDOLEN
Mrs. Kowalski-Asante, what is the attendance?

FATIMA
Eleven.

GWENDOLEN
Out of?

FATIMA
Sixty.

GWENDOLEN
And the rest?

FATIMA
Gone to the city.

GWENDOLEN
All forty-nine?

FATIMA
All of them. With their parents.

GWENDOLEN
Then we have a school with eleven pupils, a caretaker, a teacher, and a headmistress.

ABEBE
And a globe.

GWENDOLEN
And a globe.

A knock at the door. They freeze. Another knock, small and polite.

The door opens. A BOY of nine, in oversized shoes, stands on the threshold with a lunch pail.

BOY
Is this where school is?

Gwendolen rises, slowly, and smooths her skirt.

GWENDOLEN
It is.

BOY
Am I late?

GWENDOLEN
No. You are the twelfth.
